# Expiry date of the npm publish token stored as the GitHub Actions secret
# NPM_TOKEN on the `npm-publish` environment of Rockhopper-Co/mcp-server.
#
# WHAT THE TOKEN IS
#   A granular npm access token, publish-only, scoped to the single package
#   @rockhopper-co/mcp-server. It exists because Trusted Publishing (OIDC)
#   mints a credential that can `npm publish` and NOTHING ELSE — the release
#   pipeline's promotion step runs `npm dist-tag add`, which answers E401 under
#   OIDC (measured 2026-08-19, run 32227933905). Without this token, promoting
#   a staging build to `latest` fails and the release stops.
#
# WHAT BREAKS WHEN IT EXPIRES
#   Publishing still works (that is OIDC). Promotion — moving the `latest`
#   dist-tag onto an already-published version — fails. In practice: every
#   production release from `main` stops until the token is replaced.
#
# HOW TO ROTATE
#   1. npmjs.com -> Access Tokens -> Generate New Token -> Granular Access
#      Token. Packages: @rockhopper-co/mcp-server only. Permission:
#      Read and write. Expiration: 90 days.
#   2. Store the value in 1Password, vault "Rockhopper Engineering", item
#      title NPM_TOKEN_MCP_SERVER. Never paste it into a shell, a file, or a
#      chat.
#   3. GitHub -> Rockhopper-Co/mcp-server -> Settings -> Environments ->
#      npm-publish -> Environment secrets -> NPM_TOKEN -> update.
#   4. Update the date on the last line of this file to the new expiry and
#      push. That is what silences the weekly warning.
#   5. Prove the new token works before trusting it:
#        gh workflow run publish.yml --ref dev -f verify_promotion_credential=true
#      That probe adds and immediately removes a throwaway dist-tag and asserts
#      `latest` and `staging` never moved.
#
# READ BY: .github/workflows/npm-token-expiry.yml (weekly). Lines starting with
# `#` and blank lines are ignored; the first remaining line is the date, in
# YYYY-MM-DD.
